\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Since no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are only \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, hence at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nThus any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.1 Sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + i-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n#### 2.2 Non‑sunny lines  \n\nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j ,\\qquad\nj=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf \\(\\displaystyle m-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{m+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor>0\\), add that many further distinct lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (e.g. \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots\\)).  \nAll added lines are non‑sunny and distinct from the lines in (2) and (3).  \nAltogether we have exactly \\(m=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Proof that the \\(n\\) constructed lines cover \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).*  \nThen \\(b-a