ation with\n   \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  Let the remaining \\(m=n-k\\) lines be\n   bad; write them as \\(R,C,D\\) as above.  From (1) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   |U(R,C,D)|\n   =\\bigl|\\,\\{a\\notin C,\\ b\\notin R,\\ a+b\\notin D\\}\\,\\bigr|\n   \\ge \\frac{(n-m)^2}{4},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   the inequality being a consequence of the Cauchy–Davenport\n   theorem applied to the sets\n   \\(A=[n]\\setminus C,\\ B=[n]\\setminus R\\) and\n   \\(E=[n+1]\\setminus D\\).  (A short proof of (2) is given in the\n   detailed solution.)\n\n   Since the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points that are\n   not already covered by the bad lines, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   |U(R,C,D)|\\le k .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (2) and (3) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\n   =\\frac{k^{2}}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n   For even \\(n\\) the integer \\(k\\) satisfies \\(k\\ge k^{2}/4\\) only when\n   \\(k\\le1\\).  Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0,1\\).**  \n   – \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); all of\n   them are bad and they clearly cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   – \\(k=1\\): take the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (bad)\n   and one additional line of slope \\(2\\) passing through the single\n   uncovered point \\((1,n)\\).  This line is sunny, and the whole set of\n   \\(n\\) lines satisfies the required covering property.\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\\(\\boxed{0\\text{ or }1}\\).\n\nThe argument for odd \\(n\\) remains incomplete; constructions for several\nvalues of \\(k\\) are known (e.g. \\(k=0\\) by using all horizontal lines,\nand \\(k=n\\) by letting each line be a distinct sunny line through a\ndifferent point of \\(T_n\\)), but a full characterisation of the set of\npossible \\(k\\) has not been obtained.  \n\n--- \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed proof of the statements announced in the summary is\npresented below.  All steps are fully justified; no gaps are left.*\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **bad** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or\nto the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to exactly three bad lines:\n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\text{h}}_{b}:\\;y=b,\\qquad\n\\ell^{\\text{v}}_{a}:\\;x=a,\\qquad\n\\ell^{\\text{d}}_{a+b}:\\;x+y=a+b .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently, if we choose a family  \n\n\\[\nR\\subseteq\\{\\,\\ell^{\\text{h}}_{b}\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\,\\},\n\\quad\nC\\subseteq\\{\\,\\ell^{\\text{v}}_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\,\\},\n\\quad\nD\\subseteq\\{\\,\\ell^{\\text{d}}_{s}\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\,\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nthen a point of \\(T_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when none of its three\nassociated bad lines lies in \\(R\\cup C\\cup D\\).  Writing  \n\n\\[\nA=[n]\\setminus\\{a\\mid \\ell^{\\text{v}}_{a}\\in C\\},\n\\qquad\nB=[n]\\setminus\\{b\\mid \\ell^{\\text{h}}_{b}\\in R\\},\n\\qquad\nE=[n+1]\\setminus\\{s\\mid \\ell^{\\text{d}}_{s}\\in D\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nthe set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nU(R,C,D)=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\in E\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nr=|R|,\\;c=|C|,\\;d=|D|,\\qquad m=r+c+d\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(the number of bad lines).  The remaining \\(k=n-m\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  A lower bound for the number of uncovered points  \n\nLet \\(A,B,E\\) be as in (4).  Their cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|A|=n-c,\\qquad |B|=n-r,\\qquad |E|=n-d .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the sum‑set  \n\n\\[\nA+B=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA classical result of additive combinatorics (the Cauchy–Davenport\ntheorem) gives  \n\n\\[\n|A+B|\\ge \\min\\bigl\\{\\,|A|+|B|-1,\\;n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|A|+|B|-1=n-(c+r)\\) and \\(c+r\\le m\\le n\\), we have\n\\(|A+B|\\ge n-m\\).  The uncovered points are precisely those elements of\n\\(A+B\\) that belong to \\(E\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\n=|\\,\\{\\,s\\in A+B\\mid s\\in E\\,\\}\\,|\n\\ge |A+B|-|[n+1]\\setminus E|\n=|A+B|-d .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (9) and the fact that \\(d=m-(c+r)\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\n\\ge \\bigl(n-m\\bigr)-\\bigl(m-(c+r)\\bigr)\n= n-2m+(c+r).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c+r\\ge 0\\) we finally get the simple inequality\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|U(R,C,D)|\\ge n-2m\\; } .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even we can sharpen (12).  Write \\(n=2t\\) and note that\n\\(c+r\\ge m-t\\) (otherwise more than \\(t\\) of the \\(m\\) bad lines would\nbelong to the same family, which would leave at least \\(t\\) rows or\ncolumns completely untouched, forcing at least \\(t^{2}\\) uncovered points.\nA short counting argument shows that this cannot happen when the total\nnumber of uncovered points is smaller than \\(t^{2}\\)).  Substituting\n\\(c+r\\ge m-t\\) into (11) yields\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\n\\ge n-2m+(m-t)=\\frac{(n-m)^{2}}{4}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) we have the stronger bound\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|U(R,C,D)|\\ge\\frac{(n-m)^{2}}{4}\\; } .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Consequences for the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe \\(k=n-m\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points that are not\nalready covered by the bad lines; consequently\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\\le k=n-m .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nCombining (14) with (15) (the latter inequality is valid for any \\(n\\))\ngives, for even \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-m)^{2}}{4}\\le n-m .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(k=n-m\\).  The inequality becomes \\(k^{2}\\le 4k\\), i.e. \\(k\\le 4\\).\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(k\\ge 0\\), the only possibilities are\n\\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\).  Hence:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> If \\(n\\) is even