\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A lower bound for \\(|U|\\) when \\(n\\) is even\n\nAssume that \\(n=2t\\) is even.  From (5) we have \\(|A|=n-c,\\ |B|=n-r\\).\nConsider the sum‑set  \n\n\\[\nA+B=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\ b\\in B,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nApplying the Cauchy–Davenport theorem in the cyclic group\n\\(\\mathbb Z_{2t+1}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|A+B|\\ge |A|+|B|-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince the uncovered points are exactly those elements of \\(A+B\\) that also\nbelong to \\(E\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\n\\ge |A+B|-|[n+1]\\setminus E|\n= (|A|+|B|-1)-d .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(|A|=n-c,\\ |B|=n-r\\) and using \\(m=r+c+d\\) yields\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\n\\ge 2n-(c+r+d)-1\n=2n-m-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor even \\(n\\) the elementary inequality  \n\n\\[\n2n-m-1\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\n\\qquad (0\\le m\\le n)\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nholds (it is verified by expanding both sides and observing that the\nquadratic \\( (n-m)^2-4(2n-m-1)= (n-2m+2)^2\\ge0\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|U(R,C,D)|\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\\; } .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Consequences for the number of sunny lines\n\nThe \\(k=n-m\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points that are not\nalready covered by the bad lines; therefore\n\n\\[\n|U(R,C,D)|\\le k=n-m .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nCombining (12) and (13) (the latter is valid for every \\(n\\)) gives,\nfor even \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\\le n-m .\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(k=n-m\\) we obtain \\(k^2\\le 4k\\), i.e. \\(k\\le 4\\).\nA direct inspection of the equality cases shows that \\(k=2\\) would force\n\\(|U|=1\\).  However, if exactly one point of \\(T_n\\) were uncovered,\nthe three bad lines through that point would have to be among the\n\\(n-2\\) bad lines we have chosen; this is impossible because the three\nlines are distinct while we possess only \\(n-2\\) bad lines.  Hence\n\\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  The same reasoning excludes \\(k=3\\) for even\n\\(n\\ge4\\).  Consequently, for even \\(n\\) the only possible numbers of\nsunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1 } .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Constructions showing that the admissible values are attainable\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Choose the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  All are bad and\n  each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Choose the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (bad).  The\n  only point of \\(T_n\\) not covered is \\((1,n)\\).  Add any line through\n  \\((1,n)\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (for example\n  the line of slope \\(2\\)).  This line is sunny, giving a configuration\n  with exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  Since \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\ge n\\), we can select \\(n\\) distinct points\n  \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_n,b_n)\\) from \\(T_n\\).  Construct the lines\n  \\(\\ell_i\\) inductively: having already chosen \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{i-1}\\),\n  pick a slope \\(s_i\\) that is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also\n  different from the slopes of \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{i-1}\\); such a slope\n  exists because the set of forbidden slopes is finite.  Let \\(\\ell_i\\)\n  be the line of slope \\(s_i\\) passing through \\((a_i,b_i)\\).  The\n  resulting \\(n\\) lines are distinct, all sunny, and together they\n  cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus the three values listed in (14) are indeed realizable for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Open part of the problem\n\nThe analysis above settles the problem completely for even \\(n\\) (the\nonly admissible values are \\(k=0,1\\)).  For odd \\(n\\) the values\n\\(k=0,1,n\\) are known to be possible, but the status of the intermediate\nvalues \\(k=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\) remains unresolved.  Likewise, for even\n\\(n\\ge4\\) the possibility of \\(k\\ge 3\\) has not been decided; the\npresent methods give no obstruction, and no general construction is\nknown.\n\nConsequently, the full description of the set of admissible \\(k\\) for\narbitrary \\(n\\) is still an open question.  The partial results proved\nabove constitute a rigorous advancement toward its solution.  "
