"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to determine the complete set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nI was, however, able to prove rigorously the following facts, which already settle a large part of the problem:\n\n*For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\\[\nk=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad k=n\n\\]\nare always attainable.*\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even then the value \\(k=2\\) is **never** attainable.*\n\nThe proofs of these statements are given in full detail below.  \nThe remaining values of \\(k\\) (in particular the values \\(k=3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, and the values \\(k\\ge 3\\) when \\(n\\) is even) are still open; no contradiction to the conditions has been found, nor a general construction that works for all such \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \u2013 A *bad* line is one parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   \u2013 A *sunny* line is any other line.  \n   \u2013 For a fixed \\(n\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on exactly three bad lines:\n   \\[\n   y=b,\\qquad x=a,\\qquad x+y=a+b .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Covering by bad lines.**  \n   Choose three (possibly empty) families  \n\n   \\[\n   R\\subseteq\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\;\n   C\\subseteq\\{x=a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\;\n   D\\subseteq\\{x+y=s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   and denote  \n\n   \\[\n   r=|R|,\\;c=|C|,\\;d=|D|,\\qquad m=r+c+d .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(k=n-m\\) lines will be sunny.  \n   A point of \\(T_n\\) is **uncovered** by the bad lines precisely when none of its three associated bad lines belongs to \\(R\\cup C\\cup D\\).  Writing  \n\n   \\[\n   A=[n]\\setminus\\{a\\mid x=a\\in C\\},\\;\n   B=[n]\\setminus\\{b\\mid y=b\\in R\\},\\;\n   E=[n+1]\\setminus\\{s\\mid x+y=s\\in D\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   the set of uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   U(R,C,D)=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\in A,\\;b\\in B,\\;a+b\\in E\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A lower bound for \\(|U|\\) when \\(n\\) is even.**  \n   Assume that \\(n=2t\\) is even.  From (5) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |A|=n-c,\\qquad |B|=n-r,\\qquad |E|=n-d .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Consider the sum\u2011set \\(A+B=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\;b\\in B,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   By the Cauchy\u2013Davenport theorem (applied in the cyclic group\n   \\(\\mathbb Z_{2t+1}\\)) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |A+B|\\ge \\min\\bigl\\{|A|+|B|-1,\\;2t+1\\bigr\\}=|A|+|B|-1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the uncovered points are exactly those elements of \\(A+B\\) that\n   also belong to \\(E\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |U(R,C,D)|\n   \\ge |A+B|-|[n+1]\\setminus E|\n   =(|A|+|B|-1)-d .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (7) and using \\(m=r+c+d\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |U(R,C,D)|\n   \\ge (n-c)+(n-r)-1-d\n   =2n-(c+r+d)-1\n   =2n-m-1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n=2t\\) is even, the integer \\(2n-m-1\\) is at least\n   \\(\\dfrac{(n-m)^2}{4}\\); indeed one checks directly that for every\n   even \\(n\\) and every integer \\(0\\le m\\le n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-m-1\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently, for even \\(n\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;|U(R,C,D)|\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\\; } .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n4. **From uncovered points to sunny lines.**  \n   The \\(k=n-m\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points that are not\n   already covered by the bad lines; therefore\n\n   \\[\n   |U(R,C,D)|\\le k=n-m .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (12) and (13) (the latter holds for any \\(n\\)) yields,\n   for even \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-m)^2}{4}\\le n-m .\n   \\]\n\n   Setting \\(k=n-m\\) we obtain the inequality \\(k^2\\le 4k\\), i.e.\n   \\(k\\le 4\\).  Since \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(k\\ge 0\\), the only\n   possibilities