(x\\)-axis).\n\nAt this point we have \\(k+2r\\) distinct lines.  \nBecause \\(r\\le\\frac{n-k}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\nk+2r\\le k+ (n-k)=n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k+2r=n\\) we stop.  \nIf \\(k+2r<n\\) we append any further non‑sunny lines (for instance the horizontal\nlines \\(y=r+1, y=r+2,\\dots\\)) until the total number of lines becomes exactly\n\\(n\\).  The added lines do not affect the covering argument that follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering proof  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThen \\(b-a=i-1\\) for a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), and by (2) the point lies on\n\\(S_i\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-(b-a)\\le n+1-k,\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad a\\le\\frac{n-k}{2}\\le r .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\le r\\)) contains\n\\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a-b\\ge k\\).  \nBy symmetry we get \\(b\\le r\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) contains\n\\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn each case the point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines, so the\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow we need a family of \\(n\\) **sunny** lines that covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = 2x + (t-n). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their\nintercepts \\(t-n\\) are different.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(- (n-1)\\le d\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(d\\) is even*, write \\(d=2q\\) with \\(-\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) and\nchoose  \n\n\\[\nt = q + \\frac{n-1}{2}\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n2a + (t