\[\nk + a + (b-1)=k+(a+b)-1 = k+(n+1-k)-1 = n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Covering proof\n\nTake any \\((p,q)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(p,q\\ge1\\) and \\(p+q\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(q-p\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThen \\((p,q)\\) satisfies \\(q = p + (q-p)\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,q-p}\\) from (2).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(q-p\\ge k\\).  \nFrom \\(p+q\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np\\le\\frac{n+1-(q-p)}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(1\\le p\\le a\\) and the vertical line \\(V_p\\) from (3) contains \\((p,q)\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(p-q\\ge k\\).  \nBy symmetry we get  \n\n\\[\nq\\le\\frac{n+1-(p-q)}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le q\\le b-1\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_q\\) from (4) contains \\((p,q)\\).\n\nIn each of the three mutually exclusive cases the point \\((p,q)\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (2)–(4). Consequently the family of \\(n\\) lines covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow all \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  \nFor \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y = 2x + (t-n) .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is sunny; the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts \\(t-n\\) are distinct.\n\nLet \\((p,q)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(d:=q-p\\).  \nFrom \\(p+q\\le n+1\\) we have \\(-\\,(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(d\\) is even*, write \\(d=2s\\) with \\(-\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le s\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) and choose  \n\n\\[\nt:=s+\\frac{n-1}{2}\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n2p+(t-n)=2p+\\Bigl(s+\\frac{n-1}{2}-n\\Bigr)\n        =2p+s-\\frac{1}{2}=p+ (p+2s)-\\frac{1}{2}=q,\n\\]\n\nso \\((p,q)\\) satisfies (6) with this \\(t\\).\n\n*If \\(d\\) is odd*, write \\(d=2s+1\\) with \\(-\\frac{n-2}{2}\\le s\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nt:=s+\\frac{n-2}{2}+1\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation gives again \\(q=2p+(t-n)\\); hence \\((p,q)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(\\{L_t\\}_{t=0}^{n-1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the construction of Sections 2.1–2.2 provides \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nFor \\(k=n\\) the construction of Section 3 supplies the required family of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTogether with the obvious upper bound (1), we have proved that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
