\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nOnly \\(n\\) lines are allowed, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{d}:\\;y=x+d .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(S_{d}\\) is \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_{d}\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\na:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad \nb:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a+b=n+1-k\\) and \\(b\\in\\{a,a+1\\}\\).\n\nDefine the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i ,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,a ,\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,b-1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (3)–(4) are parallel to the coordinate axes, hence non‑sunny.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk\\;+\\;a\\;+\\;(b-1)=k+(a+b)-1=k+(n+1-k)-1=n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct because the three families have different directions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification that the lines cover \\(T_{n}\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((p,q)\\in T_{n}\\); thus \\(p,q\\ge1\\) and \\(p+q\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(q-p\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nThen \\((p,q)\\) satisfies \\(q=p+(q-p)\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,q-p}\\) from (2).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(q-p\\ge k\\).  \nFrom \\(p+q\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np\\le\\frac{n+1-(q-p)}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le a .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(1\\le p\\le a\\) and the vertical line \\(V_{p}\\) (defined in (3)) contains \\((p,q)\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(p-q\\ge k\\).  \nAnalogously,\n\n\\[\nq\\le\\frac{n+1-(p-q)}{2}\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le q\\le b-1\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_{q}\\) (defined in (4)) contains \\((p,q)\\).\n\nThe three cases are exhaustive, so every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (2)–(4).  \nConsequently the family of \\(n\\) lines constructed above satisfies the required covering property and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow all \\(n\\) lines must be sunny.  \nWe employ two sunny slopes, \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n\\ell:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Slope 1.*  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL^{(1)}_{d}:\\;y=x+d .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Slope \\(-2\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\ell\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL^{(2)}_{t}:\\;y=-2x+(n+1+t) .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (6) and (7) are sunny (their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), respectively) and they are pairwise distinct because the intercepts are different.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(m+1)+\\ell =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n               +\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil = n .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((p,q)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n1. **If \\(0\\le q-p\\le m\\).**  \n   Then \\((p,q)\\) lies on the line \\(L^{(1)}_{\\,q-p}\\) from (6).\n\n2. **If \\(q-p\\ge m+1\\).**  \n   From \\(p+q\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   p\\le\\frac{n+1-(q-p)}{2}\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(p\\le m\\).  Write  \n\n   \\[\n   t:=q-p-m\\quad(\\ge1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(q-p\\le n-1\\), we have \\(t\\le\\ell\\).  Substituting \\(t\\) into (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   -2p+(n+1+t)= -2p+n+1+q-p-m = q ,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((p,q)\\) lies on \\(L^{(2)}_{t}\\).\n\n3. **If \\(p-q\\ge1\\).**  \n   Interchanging the roles of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) in the previous argument shows that \\((p,q)\\) also belongs to a line of the form (7).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is contained in at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines (6)–(7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause (1) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
