"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   At most \\(n\\) lines are available, therefore \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   *Vertical lines.*  Put  \n\n   \\[\n   V:=n-k,\\qquad L_{i}:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,V).\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(V\\) lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   d\\in D:=\\{-V,\\,-V+1,\\dots , -1,\\,0\\}\n   \\]\n\n   define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{d}:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(S_{d}\\) have slope \\(1\\); hence they are sunny.  \n   The set \\(D\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) integers because  \n\n   \\[\n   |D|=V+1=n-k+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |D|-1=k .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently we keep the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S:=\\{S_{d}\\mid d\\in D\\setminus\\{0\\}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the line \\(y=x\\) is omitted when \\(k<n\\); when \\(k=n\\) the whole family is used, see step\u202f3).  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   V+|\\mathcal S|= (n-k)+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le V\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(L_{a}\\).  \n\n   * If \\(a>V\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(V+1)=k .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and  \n\n     \\[\n     d:=b-a\\in\\{-V,\\dots ,-1\\}=D\\setminus\\{0\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line\n     \\(S_{d}\\).  \n\n   Thus every required point belongs to at least one of the constructed\n   lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   Write  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n   t:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   *Family\u202f\\(A\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).*  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   A_{d}:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\]\n\n   *Family\u202f\\(B\\) (slope\u202f\\(-2\\)).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   c_{0}:=m+4,\\qquad\n   C:=\\{c_{0},c_{0}+1,\\dots ,c_{0}+t-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{c}:\\;y=-2x+c .\n   \\]\n\n   All lines in \\(A\\cup B\\) have slopes \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\); therefore none\n   of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n   \\(x+y=0\\).  The families are disjoint because the slopes differ, and\n   the intercepts are distinct, so the lines are distinct.  Their total\n   number is  \n\n   \\[\n   |A|+|B|=(m+1)+t=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Coverage proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) be a point with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and set\n   \\(d:=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f1 \u2013 \\(d\\ge 0\\).*  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(d\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(A_{d}\\).  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(d\\ge m+1\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     c:=b+2a=3a+d .\n     \\]\n\n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(2a+d\\le n+1\\), i.e.  \n\n     \\[\n     a\\le\\frac{n+1-d}{2}.\n     \\]\n\n     Consequently  \n\n     \\[\n     c=3a+d\\le 3\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr)+d\n       =\\frac{3n+3-d}{2}\\le c_{0}+t-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Moreover \\(a\\ge1\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     c=3a+d\\ge 3+d\\ge m+4=c_{0}.\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(c\\in C\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the line\n     \\(B_{c}\\).\n\n   *Case\u202f2 \u2013 \\(d<0\\) (i.e. \\(a>b\\)).*  \n   Write \\(d' = a-b = -d>0\\).  The same argument as in Case\u202f1, with the\n   roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) interchanged, shows that either\n   \\(d'\\le m\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=x-d'\\) (which is the\n   same geometric line as \\(A_{d'}\\)), or \\(d'\\ge m+1\\) and the point\n   satisfies a line of family \\(B\\) with in