"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible (obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base configurations for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Explicit families of three lines are exhibited that realise the four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n2. **Inductive construction.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \n   *To obtain a family for \\(n+1\\) and the same \\(k\\):* add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) (which is not sunny) \u2013 it covers the new lattice point \\((n+1,1)\\) and does not duplicate any previous line.  \n   *To obtain a family for \\(n+1\\) and \\(k+1\\):* start from the family for \\(n\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines, add the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) as above, and then replace this vertical line by a sunny line through \\((n+1,1)\\) whose slope is chosen different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of all existing lines. This raises the number of sunny lines by one while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n+1\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   By induction the construction works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Since a line cannot be both sunny and non\u2011sunny, no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so the list above is complete.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe following families of three distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and realise the four possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of lines | remarks |\n|------|-----------------|---------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | all vertical (non\u2011sunny) |\n| 1 | \\(y=x,\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\) | only \\(y=x\\) is sunny |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y-2=x,\\;y-3=2x\\) | the last two lines have slopes \\(1\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\) (sunny) |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y-2=\\tfrac12(x-1),\\;y-3=2(x-1)\\) | all three slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) |\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for a fixed \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe show how to obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Keeping the same number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) be as above.  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{new}= \\{(x,y)\\mid x=n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe only new lattice point that appears in \\(T_{n+1}\\) but not in \\(T_n\\) is \\((n+1,1)\\); it lies on \\(L_{new}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) already belong to \\(T_n\\) and are covered by the original \\(n\\) lines.  \n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{new}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly the same \\(k\\) sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nStart again from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (with \\(k\\le n\\)).  \nAdd the vertical line \\(L_{new}\\) as in (2).  \nNow replace \\(L_{new}\\) by a line  \n\n\\[\nL_{sun}= \\{(x,y)\\mid y=m(x-(n+1))+1\\},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere the slope \\(m\\) is chosen so that  \n\n* \\(m\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq -1\\) (so \\(L_{sun}\\) is sunny);\n* \\(m\\) is different from the slopes of all lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (this is possible because only finitely many slopes are forbidden).\n\nThe line \\(L_{sun}\\) passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\) and is distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) are covered: the old points are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), and \\((n+1,1)\\) is covered by \\(L_{sun}\\).  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{sun}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) supplies configurations for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nApplying the two constructions of Sections\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2 repeatedly, we obtain for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in any admissible configuration; consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe constructions above show that every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable. Hence no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
