"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous proof that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) remains unproved; the constructions that would bridge the gap encounter a difficulty in covering the new lattice points that appear when the size of the triangle is increased.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x+i=0\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   i.e. the vertical lines \\(x=-1,-2,\\dots ,-n\\).  \n   After a translation of the whole picture by the vector \\((n+2,0)\\) these become the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \n   Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   All lines are vertical, therefore none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(i\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   For a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \n   Choose \\(i=b-a+1\\); then \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) because \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\) imply \\(1\\le b-a+1\\le n\\).  \n   Substituting this \\(i\\) into (1) gives\n   \\[\n   b = (b-a+1)a + (1-(b-a+1)) = b,\n   \\]\n   so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{\\,b-a+1}\\).  \n   Hence every required lattice point belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThese two constructions settle the extremal cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  \nThe difficulty in extending the argument to intermediate values of \\(k\\) is that, when one tries to add a new line to increase the number of sunny lines, the new line must also cover all lattice points whose sum of coordinates equals the new maximal value \\(n+2\\); a single line cannot pass through all those points, and a careful arrangement of slopes is required.  A complete resolution of this issue has not been achieved in the present work.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*Let*  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (because \\(a\\le a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(i\\); since \\(i\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nWe claim that the set \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  By (3) this \\(i\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSubstituting \\(i\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = (b-a+1)x + (1-(b-a+1)).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = (b-a+1)a + (1-b+a-1)=b,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b-a+1}\\).\n\nSince the argument works for every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\), the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) indeed covers all required points and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remarks on the intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe two constructions above show that the extremal values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable.  \nFor a value \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) one would need a mixture of sunny and non\u2011sunny lines that together cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \nA natural idea is to start from the configuration for \\(k=0\\) (all vertical lines) and replace some of the vertical lines by sunny lines that still pass through the points that were previously covered.  \nThe main obstacle is that, when the size of the triangle is increased from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\), new lattice points appear on the line \\(a+b=n+2\\); a single line cannot pass through all of them, so a careful redistribution of slopes is required.  \nAt present a general method that works for every intermediate \\(k\\) has not been found, and the existence of such configurations remains an open question.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k=n\n\\]\n\nare realizable.  \nThe existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been established in this solution."
