ning it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,2) & y=2x-1 \\;(S_{2})\\\\\n(1,3) & y=2x-1 \\;(S_{2})\\\\\n(2,1) & y=\\tfrac12x-\\tfrac12 \\;(S_{3})\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nHence every element of \\(T_{3}\\) belongs to at least one of the three sunny lines, giving a configuration with \\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Existence for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse two vertical (non‑sunny) lines and one sunny line:\n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:x=2,\\qquad\nS: y=\\tfrac12 x-\\tfrac12 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so it is sunny.  \n\n* Points with \\(a=1\\) (\\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)) lie on \\(L_{1}\\).  \n* Points with \\(a=2\\) (\\((2,1),(2,2)\\)) lie on \\(L_{2}\\).  \n* The remaining point \\((3,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=\\tfrac12\\cdot3-\\tfrac12\\) and therefore lies on \\(S\\).\n\nThus all six points are covered and exactly one of the three lines is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that three distinct lines cover \\(T_{3}\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nConsequently one of the lines is **non‑sunny**, i.e. it is either\n\n* vertical \\(x=c\\),\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\), or\n* of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=d\\).\n\nWe treat each possibility and show that at least three sunny lines would be required, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny line is vertical \\(x=c\\)\n\nBecause the points of \\(T_{3}\\) have abscissae \\(1,2,3\\), we have \\(c\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  The line covers \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Any pair among these three has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  \n  Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed – impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line covers \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  The four uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only pair among them whose slope is sunny is \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  Consequently one sunny line can contain at most these two points; the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) cannot be placed on a sunny line (they are vertically aligned).  \n  Thus a third sunny line is unavoidable.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The line covers only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  The five remaining points each have abscissa \\(1\\) or \\(2\\).  \n  Any sunny line can contain at most two of them (the only sunny pair is \\((1,2)\\) with \\((3,1)\\), which is already used).  \n  Consequently at least three sunny lines are required.\n\nIn every sub‑case a configuration with exactly two sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The non‑sunny line is horizontal \\(y=c\\)\n\nThe situation is symmetric to the vertical case (exchange the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)).  \nThe same three sub‑cases (\\(c=1,2,3\\)) lead to the same contradictions, so a horizontal non‑sunny line also forces at least three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  The non‑sunny line has slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=d\\))\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le4\\) for points of \\(T_{3}\\), the possible values of \\(d\\) are \\(2,3,4\\).\n\n* **\\(d=2\\).**  \n  The line contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  The five other points must be covered by two sunny lines, which is impossible because a sunny line can contain at most two of them (any three of the five contain a pair of slope \\(0,-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\n* **\\(d=3\\).**  \n  The line contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n  The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Among these, the only sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  After using a sunny line for that pair, the points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) cannot be placed together on a sunny line (their slope is \\(-1\\)).  \n  Hence a third sunny line would be needed.\n\n* **\\(d=4\\).**  \n  The line contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The three uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  No two of them have a sunny slope (the three possible slopes are \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)).  \n  Therefore each would require its own sunny line, i.e. three sunny lines.\n\nThus a line of slope \\(-1\\) also forces at least three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice of the unique non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe exhaustive analysis yields\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\n\\]\n\nas the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the possible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=3}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
