 therefore a configuration that satisfies the covering condition cannot contain both a sunny line and a non\u2011sunny line.  Consequently the number of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny) or \\(n\\) (all lines sunny).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The two explicit constructions show that both extremal values are attainable, and the impossibility argument shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Extremal configurations\n\n#### 1.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nAll the lines are vertical, therefore none of them is sunny.  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); since \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le b-a+1\\le n .\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this value of \\(i\\) into (1) gives the equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x + (b-a).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the line \\(L_i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).\n\nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No mixed configurations are possible\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing **both** a sunny line and a non\u2011sunny line.  We treat the three possible types of non\u2011sunny lines separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A non\u2011sunny vertical line is present\n\nSuppose \\(x=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(c\\) are covered by this line.  \nEvery remaining point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\neq c\\); therefore its abscissa is different from that of any other point already covered by the vertical line.  \n\nA sunny line has a finite (non\u2011vertical) slope, so it can contain at most one point with a given abscissa.  Consequently, to cover all points whose abscissa is not \\(c\\) we would need at least one distinct line for each of the \\(n-1\\) remaining abscissae.  Since \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains only \\(n-1\\) further lines, the only way to achieve the covering is that **all** those lines are also vertical, i.e. non\u2011sunny.  This contradicts the assumption that a sunny line is present.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  A non\u2011sunny horizontal line is present\n\nThe argument is symmetric to the previous one.  If the line \\(y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\), then every point with ordinate \\(c\\) is covered.  Any other point has a different ordinate, and a sunny line can meet a given horizontal line in at most one point.  Hence all the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would have to be horizontal as well, contradicting the existence of a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  A non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) is present\n\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) consists of all points with a fixed sum \\(a+b\\).  \nLet such a line be \\(a+b=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  All points of the anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) are covered by this line.\n\nConsider any other line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  If \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, its slope is different from \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) can intersect the anti\u2011diagonal \\(a+b=s\\) in **at most one** point.  Consequently, after the removal of the anti\u2011diagonal, the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) can be covered only if every other line is also of slope \\(-1\\) (otherwise some point of the anti\u2011diagonal would remain uncovered).  This again contradicts the presence of a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince each possible type of non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain both a sunny line and a non\u2011sunny line.  Hence the number of sunny lines in any admissible configuration is either\n\n* \\(0\\) (all lines are non\u2011sunny), or\n* \\(n\\) (all lines are sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the classification\n\nThe constructions of Sections\u202f1.1 and\u202f1.2 show that both possibilities actually occur.  By the impossibility proved in Section\u202f2, no other value of \\(k\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,n\\}\\).**  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k=n\\;}\n\\]"

