r all \\(a\\neq c\\) gives a total of at least\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)- (n+1-c)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-c)\n   \\]\n   distinct non‑vertical lines, which is larger than \\(n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\).  Hence a configuration containing a vertical line cannot also contain a sunny line.\n\n   *Case B – a non‑sunny horizontal line.*  \n   The argument is symmetric: a horizontal line covers a whole row, and every other row would require at least as many non‑horizontal lines as its length, which again exceeds the available number of lines when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n   *Case C – a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\).*  \n   Such a line is an anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \n   All points of that anti‑diagonal must be covered.  \n   A sunny line can intersect the anti‑diagonal in at most one point (its slope is not \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently each point of the anti‑diagonal would need a distinct sunny line, i.e. at least \\(s-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since \\(s-1\\ge 2\\) for every admissible \\(s\\) and we have at most \\(n-1\\) remaining lines, the covering is impossible for \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n   In every possible type of non‑sunny line the presence of a sunny line leads to a contradiction.  Therefore a feasible family of \\(n\\) lines either consists entirely of non‑sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)) or entirely of sunny lines (\\(k=n\\)).  \n\n4. **The special case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the above counting arguments fail because the columns are short enough to allow mixed families.  Direct constructions give all four possibilities:\n   * \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\): vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and a sunny line through \\((3,1)\\);\n   * \\(k=2\\): the non‑sunny horizontal line \\(y=1\\) together with two sunny lines through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\);\n   * \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) through \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).\n\n   No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because we have exactly three lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions exhibit that the values listed in the statement are attainable, and the impossibility proof shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe are asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Constructions\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=1\\) (all \\(n\\ge 3\\))\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and add a single sunny line that passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of the right‑most column.  \nAny slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) gives a sunny line; for definiteness we may use  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y = \\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with abscissa \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines, while \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(S\\).  Hence \\(k=1\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x-(i-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); because \\(i\\ge 1\\) we have \\(i\\neq 0,-1\\), and the line is not vertical, so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nAll the lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nFix a column \\(a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) in (2) yields the set of ordinates\n\n\\[\n\\{\\,i a-(i-1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n   =\\{\\, (i-1)(a-1)+a \\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(i\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) the expression \\((i-1)(a-1)+a\\) runs through the arithmetic progression  \n\n\\[\na,\\; a+(a-1),\\; a+2(a-1),\\dots , a+(n-1)(a-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\le n\\) we have \\(a+(n-1)(a-1)=n+1-a\\).  Moreover the step \\(a-1\\) is a positive integer, so the progression lists **exactly** the integers  \n\n\\[\na,\\;a+1,\\;a+2,\\dots ,n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nConsequently, for the fixed column \\(a\\) the points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid b= a, a+1,\\dots ,n+1-a\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the intersection points of the column \\(x=a\\) with the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  This gives a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n#### 2.4  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the five points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nAll four values of \\(k\\) are realizable:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\);\n* \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) together with a sunny line through \\((3,1)\\);\n* \\(k=2\\): the non‑sunny horizontal line \\(y=1\\) together with the sunny lines\n  \\[\n  y-2 = 1\\,(x-1)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad y-2 = 2\\,(x-1),\n  \\]\n  which pass through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) respectively;\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) through \\((1,1), (1,2), (1,3)\\).\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of mixed families when \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains **both** a sunny line and a non‑sunny line.  We treat the three possible types of non‑sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  A non‑sunny vertical line is present  \n\nLet \\(V:x=c\\) be such a line.  All points of column \\(c\\) are covered by \\(V\\).  \nConsider any other column \\(a\\neq c\\).  The column contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(a,1),\\;(a,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a,\\,n+1-a\\bigr) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line that is not vertical meets column \\(a\\) in **at most one** of the points listed in (3).  Hence to cover the \\(n+1-a\\) points of column \\(a\\) we need at least \\(n+1-a\\) distinct non‑vertical lines.  Summing this lower bound over all columns \\(a\\neq c\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{a=1\\\\ a\\neq c}}^{n}(n+1-a)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-c) .\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the right‑hand side exceeds \\(n-1\\), the number of lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) that are not the vertical line \\(V\\).  Consequently a configuration containing a vertical line cannot also contain a sunny line when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  A non‑sunny horizontal line is present  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  If \\(H:y=c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\), then row \\(c\\) is completely covered.  Any other row \\(b\\neq c\\) contains \\(n+1-b\\) points, and a non‑horizontal line can meet that row in at most one point.  The same counting as in §3.1 shows that for \\(n\\ge 4\\) the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are insufficient, so a horizontal line cannot coexist with a sunny line.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  A non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) is present  \n\nSuch a line is an anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s,\\qquad 2\\le s\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_s\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).  A sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it can intersect \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point.  To cover the whole anti‑diagonal we would need at least \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) distinct sunny lines.  Since \\(s-1\\ge 2\\) for every admissible \\(s\\) and we have at most \\(n-1\\) lines besides \\(D_s\\), the covering is impossible for \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.4  Consequence  \n\nIn each of the three cases the assumption that a sunny line coexists with a non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction.  Hence, for \\(n\\ge 4\\), any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines is **either**  \n\n* entirely non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\)), **or**  \n* entirely sunny (\\(k=n\\)).\n\nNo mixed value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Synthesis of all admissible values\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the explicit constructions of §2.4 give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n* For every \\(n\\ge 4\\) the constructions of §§2.1, 2.2 and 2.3 provide families with \\(k=0,1,n\\); the impossibility proof of §3 shows that no other \\(k\\) is possible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible integers \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,n\\},   & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
