\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nIf a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two requirements of the problem, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) obviously satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus only the integers in the interval \\([0,n]\\) can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Statement to be proved\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\nWe prove the claim by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nFour families, each containing three distinct lines, realise the four possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\qquad x=2,\\qquad x=3 .\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=1\\): the two vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  y = x-2\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(y=x-2\\) passes through \\((3,1)\\); the two vertical lines cover the remaining points.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y = x\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\\qquad\n  y = -2x+5\\qquad(\\text{slope }-2).\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(y=x\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); the line \\(y=-2x+5\\) contains \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\); the vertical line \\(x=1\\) covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y = x,\\qquad y = -2x+5,\\qquad y = x-2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Their union contains all six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) the claim is true for the integer \\(n-1\\); i.e. for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines satisfying (i)\u2013(ii) for the set \\(S_{n-1}\\).\n\nWe shall construct from \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\) families for \\(S_n\\) with \\(k\\) and with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Adding a non\u2011sunny line\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}^{\\;(\\text{ns})}= \\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x=n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe added line \\(x=n\\) is vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) either belongs to \\(S_{n-1}\\) (and is therefore covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\)) or has abscissa \\(a=n\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the new line \\(x=n\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}^{\\;(\\text{ns})}\\) satisfies (i) for \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\).  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Adding a sunny line\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k+1}^{\\;(\\text{s})}= \\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y = x-(n-1)\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y = x-(n-1)\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is therefore sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nAgain, points of \\(S_{n-1}\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\setminus S_{n-1}\\) must have \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=1\\).  \nSubstituting \\(a=n\\) into the equation of the added line gives  \n\n\\[\nb = n-(n-1)=1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((n,1)\\) lies on the new sunny line.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k+1}^{\\;(\\text{s})}\\) also satisfies (i) for \\(S_n\\), and it contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n-1,k}\\) plus the newly added one).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Completion of the induction\n\nStarting from the families \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) (\\(k=0,1,2,3\\)) given in the base case, we may apply the two constructions (2) and (3) repeatedly.  \nEach application increases the total number of lines by one, while preserving the number of sunny lines or increasing it by exactly one.  \nConsequently, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfying the required properties.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Non\u2011existence of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nBecause any admissible family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  \nThus, by (1), no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]"
