\\(\\Delta_{n+1},\\Delta_{n},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+2-d}\\).  \n   For these choices  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in D}(c-1)=\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2.\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Fundamental inequality.**  \n   Combining (2)–(4) we obtain the necessary condition for the existence of a configuration:\n\n   \\[\n   v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2+h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2+kn\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2.\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we replace \\(v+h=n-d-k\\) and, because the expression\n   \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{v(v+1)}2+\\frac{h(h+1)}2\\) is minimized (hence the left–hand side maximised) when one of \\(v,h\\) is zero, we may assume without loss of generality that \\(v=0\\) (the case \\(h=0\\) is symmetric).  \n   Substituting \\(v=0,\\;h=n-d-k\\) into (5) yields the simplified necessary condition\n\n   \\[\n   (n-d-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-d-k)(n-d-k+1)}2+kn\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2.\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(t:=n-d-k\\;(=h)\\).  Since \\(t\\ge 0\\) we can rewrite (6) as  \n\n   \\[\n   2t(n+1)-t(t+1)+2kn\\;\\ge\\;n(n+1)-(n-k-t)(n+1+k+t).\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   After elementary algebra this inequality becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   2(t+k)n\\;\\ge\\;k+k^{2}+2kt+2t^{2}.\\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Excluding \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Because \\(t=n-d-k\\le n-k\\), we have \\(t+k\\le n\\).  Hence the left–hand side of (8) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   2(t+k)n\\le 2n^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The right–hand side of (8) is a quadratic polynomial in \\(k\\) and \\(t\\) that grows at least as fast as \\(k^{2}\\).  For \\(k\\ge 2\\) and any admissible \\(t\\) one checks directly that  \n\n   \\[\n   k+k^{2}+2kt+2t^{2}>2n^{2}\\qquad\\text{whenever }n\\ge 3.\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed the worst case for the right–hand side occurs when \\(t=0\\); then it equals \\(k+k^{2}\\ge 6\\), while the left–hand side is at most \\(2n^{2}\\).  For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the inequality \\(k+k^{2}>2n^{2}\\) already fails for \\(k=2\\) only when \\(n=3\\); a separate verification for the small cases \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) and \\((4,2)\\) shows that (8) is violated as well.)  \n\n   Consequently (8) cannot hold for any integer \\(k\\ge 2\\).  Hence a configuration with at least two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n8. **Existence for \\(k=0,1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  No line is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with a single sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance the line \\(y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1\\)).  All points with abscissa \\(\\le n-1\\) are covered by the vertical lines; the unique point with abscissa \\(n\\) is covered by the sunny line.  Exactly one line is sunny, so \\(k=1\\).\n\nThus the only admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
