ew as possible (which makes the existence of a configuration easiest), we let the \\(d\\) diagonal lines coincide with the **largest** diagonals, i.e. with \\(\\Delta_{n+1},\\Delta_{n},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+2-d}\\).  \n   Their total contribution is\n\n   \\[\n   C_{D}(d)=\\sum_{t=n+2-d}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Necessary inequality.**  \n   All required points must be covered, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   C_{\\text{non\u2011diag}}(S,k)+C_{D}(d)\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(S=v+h=n-d-k\\) from (1) and substituting (4) and (5) into (6) we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   (n-d-k)(n+1)-\\frac{(n-d-k)\\bigl(n-d-k+2\\bigr)}4\n   +kn\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2 } .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Excluding \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Set \\(S:=n-d-k\\;(=v+h\\ge 0)\\).  Inequality (7) becomes\n\n   \\[\n   S(n+1)-\\frac{S^{2}+2S}{4}+kn\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-S-k)\\bigl(2n+1-(n-S-k)\\bigr)}2 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   After elementary algebra (multiply by\u202f4 and collect terms) we obtain the equivalent inequality\n\n   \\[\n   4kn\\;\\ge\\;k^{2}+2kS+S^{2}+k .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(S=n-d-k\\le n-k\\), we have \\(S+k\\le n\\).  Hence the left\u2011hand side of (9) satisfies\n\n   \\[\n   4kn = 4n(k) \\le 4n\\,(S+k) .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this bound into (9) yields\n\n   \\[\n   4n(S+k)\\;\\ge\\;k^{2}+2kS+S^{2}+k .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is a quadratic polynomial in the non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) and \\(S\\); it is **strictly larger** than \\(4n(S+k)\\) as soon as \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   Indeed, for \\(k\\ge 2\\) we have\n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}+2kS+S^{2}+k\n   = (k+S)^{2}+k \\;\\ge\\; (k+S)^{2}+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k+S\\le n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   (k+S)^{2}+2 \\;>\\; 4n(k+S) \\qquad\\text{for all } n\\ge 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   (The inequality \\( (k+S)^{2}+2 > 4n(k+S) \\) follows from the elementary