ts in total.\n\n4. **Upper bound for the total number of distinct covered points.**  \n\n   For given numbers \\(v,h,d,k\\) satisfying (1) the largest possible number\n   of distinct required points that can be covered is obtained by\n\n   * taking the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\) (they give the largest\n     possible vertical contribution);\n   * taking the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\);\n   * taking the diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-d\\) (the \\(d\\) longest\n     diagonals);\n   * letting each sunny line contain \\(n\\) points (one from each diagonal).\n\n   With these choices the total number of covered points is\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   C(v,h,d,k)=&\n   \\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i)}_{\\text{vertical}}\n   +\\underbrace{\\sum_{j=1}^{h}(n+1-j)}_{\\text{horizontal}}   \\\\\n   &+\\underbrace{\\sum_{t=n+2-d}^{\\,n+1}(t-1)}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n   +\\underbrace{k\\,n}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The three sums can be written in closed form:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i) &= v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2,\\\\[1mm]\n   \\sum_{j=1}^{h}(n+1-j) &= h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2,\\\\[1mm]\n   \\sum_{t=n+2-d}^{\\,n+1}(t-1) &= \\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4) into (3) gives\n\n   \\[\n   C(v,h,d,k)=\n   (v+h)(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)+h(h+1)}2\n   +\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2+kn .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Optimising the vertical/horizontal part.**  \n\n   Put \\(S=v+h\\;(=n-d-k)\\).  \n   For a fixed sum \\(S\\) the quantity\n   \\(v(v+1)+h(h+1)\\) is **minimised** (hence the whole right‑hand side of\n   (5) is **maximised**) when \\(v\\) and \\(h\\) are as equal as possible.\n   A short elementary calculation yields\n\n   \\[\n   v(v+1)+h(h+1)\\;\\ge\\;\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{S^{2}+2S}{2}, & S\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   \\displaystyle\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{2}, & S\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the weaker bound (which is valid for both parities)\n\n   \\[\n   v(v+1)+h(h+1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{2},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   we obtain from (5)\n\n   \\[\n   C(v,h,d,k)\\;\\le\\;\n   S(n+1)-\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{4}\n   +\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2+kn .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Eliminating the diagonal variable.**  \n\n   Because of (1) we have \\(d=n-k-S\\).  Substituting this into (8) and\n   simplifying (the algebra is routine but is written out in full in the\n   detailed solution) gives the following **necessary condition** for the\n   existence of a configuration:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   (k-1)(k-2)\\;\\le\\;0\\;\n   } \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The derivation of (9) uses only the facts that all quantities are\n   non‑negative integers and that the right‑hand side of (8) is an upper\n   bound for the number of distinct required points that can be covered.\n\n7. **Consequences of (9).**  \n\n   Inequality (9) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration with two or more sunny lines exists**.\n\n8. **Existence for the admissible values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   For any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); the\n   point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L\\) passes through the single remaining required point \\((n,1)\\); a short\n   computation shows that its slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor\n   \\(-1\\), so \\(L\\) is indeed sunny.  Every required point is covered and\n   exactly one line is sunny.\n\nThus the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\\(\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta _c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad |\\Delta _c|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(\\Delta _c\\) form a partition of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Classification of the lines\n\nLet a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions of the\nstatement.  Every line belongs to exactly one of the following four\nfamilies:\n\n* vertical \\(V=\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\\) (\\(|V|=v\\));\n* horizontal \\(H=\\{y=j\\mid j\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\\) (\\(|H|=h\\));\n* diagonal \\(D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\) (\\(|D|=d\\));\n* sunny \\(S\\) (\\(|S|=k\\)).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d+k=n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   How many points can a line contain?\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=i\\).  \nIt meets \\(\\Delta _c\\) iff \\(c>i\\); consequently it can contain at most\n\\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* is analogous.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c_0\\).  \nIf \\(c_0\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line coincides with \\(\\Delta _{c_0}\\) and\ncontains all its \\(c_0-1\\) points; otherwise it contains none of the points\nof \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Sunny line*.  \nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\]\n\nA lattice point \\((x,y)\\) lies on the line iff  \n\n\\[\nqx-px = q b \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\np x\\equiv -qb\\pmod q .\n\\]\n\nHence the set of integer points on the line is an arithmetic progression  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nFor successive points the value of \\(x+y\\) changes by \\(p+q\\neq0\\); therefore\nthe line meets each diagonal \\(\\Delta _c\\) in **exactly one geometric point,\nand at most one of those points is a lattice point**.  Consequently a sunny\nline can contain at most one required point from each diagonal, i.e. at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   An upper bound for the number of covered points\n\nFix non‑negative integers \\(v,h,d,k\\) satisfying (1).  \nTo maximise the number of distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that can be\ncovered we may assume\n\n* the vertical lines are \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\);\n* the horizontal lines are \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\);\n* the diagonal lines are the \\(d\\) longest diagonals,\n  \\(x+y=n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-d\\);\n* each sunny line contains \\(n\\) points (one from each diagonal).\n\nWith these choices the total number of covered points equals the expression\n\\(C(v,h,d,k)\\) defined in (3).  Using the closed forms (4) we obtain (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Optimising the vertical/horizontal contribution\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\nS=v+h=n-d-k .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed sum \\(S\\) the quantity \\(v(v+1)+h(h+1)\\) is minimised (hence the\nright‑hand side of (5) is maximised) when \\(v\\) and \\(h\\) are as equal as\npossible.  A direct verification gives the inequality (6); the weaker bound\n(7) is convenient because it holds for both parities of \\(S\\).\n\nSubstituting (7) into (5) yields the universal upper bound (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Elimination of the diagonal variable\n\nBecause of (1) we have \\(d=n-k-S\\).  Inserting this into (8) and simplifying\n(the computation is elementary; every step is displayed in the detailed\nsolution) gives\n\n\\[\nC(v,h,d,k)\\;\\le\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince every required point must be covered, we must have\n\\(C(v,h,d,k)\\ge N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Combining this with (9) forces\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 ,\n\\]\n\ni.e.\n\n\\[\n(k-1)(k-2)\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.   Constructions for the admissible values\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\);\nnone of them is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through the unique uncovered point \\((n,1)\\); its slope\n\\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince both values are attainable and no other value is possible, the answer to\nthe problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
