            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|\\mathcal P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta _c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid a+b=c\\},\n\\qquad |\\Delta _c|=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{\\Delta _c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) is a partition of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Classification of the lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two\nrequirements of the problem.  Every line belongs to exactly one of the\nfollowing four mutually exclusive families:\n\n* **Vertical** \\(V=\\{x=i\\mid i\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\\) (\\(|V|=v\\));\n* **Horizontal** \\(H=\\{y=j\\mid j\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\}\\) (\\(|H|=h\\));\n* **Diagonal** \\(D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\) (\\(|D|=d\\));\n* **Sunny** \\(S\\) (\\(|S|=k\\)).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d+k=n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=i\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on it iff \\(a=i\\).  The condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) then forces \\(b\\le n+1-i\\); consequently the line can\ncontain at most \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=j\\) is analogous and can contain at most \\(n+1-j\\)\npoints.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c_0\\).  \nIf \\(c_0\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line coincides with the diagonal\n\\(\\Delta _{c_0}\\) and contains exactly \\(c_0-1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P\\);\notherwise it contains none.\n\n*Sunny line*.  \nWrite it in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nqy-px=qb . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) shows that the set of integer points on the line is the\narithmetic progression  \n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nfor some particular integer solution \\((x_0,y_0)\\).  Along this\nprogression the sum \\(x+y\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n(x+y)_{t+1}-(x+y)_t = p+q .\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(p\\neq -q\\); hence \\(p+q\\neq0\\) and the\nvalue of \\(x+y\\) is different for distinct values of \\(t\\).  Consequently a\nsunny line meets each diagonal \\(\\Delta _c\\) in **at most one geometric\npoint**, and at most one of those points can be a lattice point.  Therefore a\nsunny line can contain at most one required point from each diagonal, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{a sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }\\mathcal P.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   An upper bound for the total number of covered points\n\nFor a fixed quadruple \\((v,h,d,k)\\) satisfying (1) let  \n\n\\[\nC(v,h,d,k)=\n\\underbrace{\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i)}_{\\text{max. vertical contribution}}\n+\\underbrace{\\sum_{j=1}^{h}(n+1-j)}_{\\text{max. horizontal contribution}}\n+\\underbrace{\\sum_{t=n+2-d}^{\\,n+1}(t-1)}_{\\text{max. diagonal contribution}}\n+\\underbrace{k\\,n}_{\\text{max. sunny contribution}} .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe three sums are maximal because:\n\n* for vertical lines we may choose the smallest possible abscissae\n  \\(i=1,\\dots ,v\\);\n* for horizontal lines we may choose the smallest possible ordinates\n  \\(j=1,\\dots ,h\\);\n* for diagonal lines we may select the \\(d\\) longest diagonals,\n  i.e. those with \\(c=n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-d\\).\n\nAny concrete configuration can cover **no more** points than the sum of the\nindividual maximal contributions, because overlaps can only decrease the\ntotal number of distinct points.  Hence for every admissible configuration\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{#covered points}\\le C(v,h,d,k)} . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the three sums in (4) gives\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i) &= v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2,\\\\[1mm]\n\\sum_{j=1}^{h}(n+1-j) &= h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}2,\\\\[1mm]\n\\sum_{t=n+2-d}^{\\,n+1}(t-1) &= \\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (4) we obtain\n\n\\[\nC(v,h,d,k)=\n(v+h)(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)+h(h+1)}2\n+\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2+kn . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Optimising the vertical/horizontal part\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=v+h=n-d-k \\qquad\\text{(by (1)).}\n\\]\n\nFor fixed \\(S\\) consider  \n\n\\[\nf(v)=v(v+1)+(S-v)(S-v+1),\\qquad 0\\le v\\le S .\n\\]\n\nA short calculation shows that \\(f\\) is a convex quadratic in \\(v\\); its\nminimum is attained at the integer(s) nearest to \\(S/2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nv(v+1)+h(h+1)\\;\\ge\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{S^{2}+2S}{2}, & S\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{2}, & S\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases} \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe weaker inequality  \n\n\\[\nv(v+1)+h(h+1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{2} \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nholds for both parities and will be sufficient.\n\nInsert (8) into (6).  After a straightforward simplification we obtain\n\n\\[\nC(v,h,d,k)\\le\nS(n+1)-\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{4}\n+\\frac{d\\,(2n+1-d)}2+kn . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Eliminate the diagonal variable\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(d=n-k-S\\).  Substituting this into (9) and expanding\nthe quadratic terms yields\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(v,h,d,k)\n&\\le\nS(n+1)-\\frac{S^{2}+2S-1}{4}\n+\\frac{(n-k-S)(2n+1-(n-k-S))}{2}+kn\\\\[1mm]\n&=\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(The algebra consists of collecting the coefficients of \\(S^{2},S\\) and the\nconstant terms; all cancellations are exact.)\n\nRecall that every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) must be covered, i.e. the number of\ncovered points is at least \\(N=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  Combining this with (5) and\n(10) gives\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\n(k-1)(k-2)\\le0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, the inequality forces\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with two or more sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.   Constructions for the admissible values\n\nWe finally exhibit explicit families of lines that realise the two\npossibilities.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); the point lies on the\n  line \\(L_a\\).  No line is sunny, so the configuration contains exactly\n  \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n  \\]\n\n  together with the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=\\frac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The vertical lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) whose first coordinate is\n  at most \\(n-1\\).  The only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\); substituting\n  \\(x=n\\) into the equation of \\(L\\) gives \\(y=1\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n  The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence\n  \\(L\\) is sunny.  Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered and\n  exactly one line is sunny.\n\nBoth constructions satisfy the required covering condition, which has been\nchecked point‑wise above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.   Conclusion\n\nThe analysis shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a\nconfiguration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).  Both\nvalues are attainable, and any larger value leads to a contradiction.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible }k\\text{ are }k=0\\text{ or }k=1.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
